    id: 'travail-2',
    titre: 'Working when your child is sick',
    contenu:
      `From 1 July 2021: parents of seriously ill children benefit from up to 14 weeks of care leave

Imagine you are working and your child suddenly falls seriously ill or has an accident. They have to stay in hospital for a long time, for several weeks or even months. Your world is turned upside down. On top of the worry for your child and the upheaval for the whole family, you sometimes have to rely on your employer's understanding in order to be able to look after your child without having to use up your holiday days.

The law provides that employers must continue to pay salary in the event of short-term absences. When a child is sick, one of the parents can take up to three consecutive paid days off, regardless of the duration of the illness. This applies to each occurrence, up to a limit of ten days per year.

Paid leave does not only cover caring for a sick or injured child, but also for other family members or one's partner.

In December 2020, Parliament approved a new regulation on care leave, which came into force on 1 July 2021.

Working parents can now benefit from a care allowance funded through the loss of earnings compensation scheme (APG/EO). Care leave can last up to 14 weeks. Parents can take it all at once or spread over several days, within an 18-month framework period.

When both parents work, the 14 weeks are shared between them. For longer absences, it remains necessary to take unpaid leave or find another care solution.

The Kind+Spital association advocates for sufficient financial support for working parents whose children are seriously ill. It notably defends parents' right to be able to accompany their sick children without suffering additional loss of income.

About Kind+Spital

Kind+Spital advocates for the rights of children and adolescents in the healthcare field. The aim is to improve the psychological and physical conditions for children and adolescents staying in hospital.`,
