    titre: 'Work and family - Equality',
    contenu:
      `How can you balance work, private life and family life? Today, more and more men and women want to pursue a professional activity without sacrificing time spent with their children and loved ones. Balancing work and family means managing to maintain a social and personal equilibrium from which everyone, as well as the company, can benefit.

However, managing household and family tasks can represent a significant burden on top of professional work, making this balance harder to achieve. Here are the 8 essential points to consider when reorganising your professional and private life.

Follow Isabelle and Nicolas step by step through their family life and discover with them the eight elements to absolutely take into account.

Nicolas and Isabelle had found their rhythm: Nicolas worked 100% to provide the family's main income, while Isabelle took care of their two children while continuing to work one day a week.

When the youngest child started school, however, Isabelle expressed the wish to work more, at 50 or 60%. The family's precious balance is then at risk of being disrupted.

### 1. Express your expectations and needs

Isabelle expresses the wish to increase her working hours. It is then Nicolas's turn to think about his own wishes: why not reduce his workload to four full days and one morning, in order to enjoy free Wednesday afternoons with the children?

### 2. Assess the couple's future income

Thanks to her increased working hours, Isabelle's income will go up. But will the family actually have more money at the end of the month? Could additional childcare costs and higher taxes reduce, or even cancel out, this extra income?

### 3. Find a childcare solution

Can you count on family, or on a day-care family? Is there an after-school care facility for the children when both Isabelle and Nicolas are working? And above all: what solution should be planned for the school holidays?

### 4. Rethink the division of household and family tasks

As Isabelle will be working more, she will have less time for household chores. Tasks therefore need to be shared among all family members, and expectations may need to be revisited. Why not occasionally or regularly hire domestic help?

### 5. Negotiate with your partner

To achieve a family and individual balance between work and family, it is necessary to think together, communicate and negotiate as a couple. Negotiation is something that can be learned. These principles can also be applied when deciding who will look after a sick child.

### 6. Consider the overall balance

Isabelle and Nicolas are ready to change their habits, but only on condition that the family's balance, as well as each individual's, is preserved. They always keep in mind the importance of having quality time with their children, as a couple, and for themselves.

### 7. Match the job to the level of qualification

Isabelle wants to advance professionally by finding a job that matches her training and qualification level. How can she highlight the skills she has acquired at home? How can she ensure her professional development and access to further training?

### 8. Negotiate with the employer

Isabelle and Nicolas, who wish to reduce their working hours, need to discuss how their schedules are organised. They also consider other possibilities: are their hours compatible with those of school and childcare facilities? How much flexibility do they have if the children are sick? Can part of the work be done from home to reduce commuting? Is it possible to organise working time on an annual basis in order to obtain more days off?`,
